[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** `strtod::parse_inf_nan` consumed the parenthesised n-char-sequence to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m place `endptr` correctly but never decoded it, always returning the default quiet
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NaN. glibc treats the sequence as a bitmask: it runs it through
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtoull(seq, &end, 0)` and honours the result *only* if that consumes the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sequence in full, then ORs the low 51 bits into the mantissa, leaving the quiet
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bit set. Base 0 is what makes the corner cases odd, and all of them were wrong
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before the fix:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `nan(010)` is 8 — a leading `0` selects octal.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `nan(08)` is 0 — `8` is not an octal digit, so the sequence is not fully
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   consumed and the payload is dropped.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `nan(0x)`, `nan(0xg)`, `nan(1e5)`, `nan(1_2)`, `nan(abc)` are all 0, for the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   same "not fully consumed" reason.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `nan(0x8000000000000)` is 0 — bit 51 is the quiet bit and falls outside the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   51-bit payload mask.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `nan(99999999999999999999999)` saturates to `ULLONG_MAX`, so the payload is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the full 51-bit mask. glibc does **not** leak the `ERANGE` that `strtoull`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   raises here; that was checked explicitly, because a leak would have turned
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   this input into a `Range error while converting base ...` in the driver.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `nan_with_payload`, `nan_payload` and `strtoull_base0` in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src/strtod.rs`; `parse_inf_nan` now decodes the sequence.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Observability.** This did **not** change the driver's output. `%.2f` prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m every NaN as `nan`/`-nan` from the sign bit alone, and `pow` propagates NaN, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout, stderr and exit status were already identical. It is recorded because
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the module is a stated reimplementation of `strtod` and was not one, and because
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the near miss above (a payload that overflows `strtoull`) *would* have been
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m visible had glibc leaked `errno`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. `main.rs` was not the translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** No output mismatch. Confirmed by rebuilding the original `main.rs`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in a scratch crate and running all 34,696 end-to-end cases against it: 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mismatches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The delivered `main.rs` declared `strtod`, `pow` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `__errno_location` as `extern "C"` and called libc directly. It therefore could
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not disagree with the C program — but `src/strtod.rs`, `src/cpow.rs` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src/cfmt.rs` were unreferenced dead code, compiled by nothing and verified by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m nothing. Defect 1 was sitting in that dead code.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `main.rs` now declares `mod cfmt; mod cpow; mod strtod;` and routes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m through them. `nm -D` confirms the binary no longer imports `strtod`; the only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m remaining libm import is `pow@GLIBC_2.29`, which is the same symbol the C binary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m imports and is how `f64::powf` is implemented on this target — so the numeric
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m result of `pow` is identical by construction, and only the `errno` reporting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m around it is reimplemented.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours confirmed correct (each one is easy to get wrong)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`""` is accepted as `0.0`.** `strtod("")` performs no conversion and leaves
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `endptr == nptr`, so `*endptr` is the terminator and the C code's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `*endptr != '\0'` test passes. `driver "" ""` prints `Result: 1.00`, exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `" "` is *rejected*, because `endptr` is reset to the start and `*endptr` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the space.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`ERANGE` is checked before the trailing-character test.** `1e400xyz`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reports `Range error while converting base`, not `Invalid numeric input`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Reversing the two checks is caught by the test suite.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`strtod` sets `ERANGE` on gradual underflow, `pow` does not.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `strtod("1e-320")` fails even though the value is a representable subnormal
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (tininess after rounding *and* inexactness), while `pow(2, -1030)` returns a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   subnormal with `errno == 0`. `pow` only reports `ERANGE` once the result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reaches zero — the cliff is at `pow(2, -1075)`. The two rules are opposite and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   both are exercised.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Underflow needs inexactness.** `strtod("0x1p-1023")` is exact, so no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ERANGE`; `strtod("1e-320")` is inexact, so `ERANGE`. The tininess threshold
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `2^-1022 - 2^-1076` hard-coded in `strtod.rs` was recomputed exactly with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `fractions.Fraction`: all 769 digits and the exponent `-307` match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`%.2f` ties.** Values like `0.125` and `-1024.875` are exact ties at the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   third decimal; glibc rounds half to even and Rust's `{:.2}` agrees. Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   differs only on NaN (`NaN` vs `nan`/`-nan`), which `cfmt.rs` special-cases.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-0.0` prints `-0.00` in both.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Domain check precedes overflow.** `pow(-2, 1e300)` is `EDOM`, not `ERANGE`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   exponents past `2^53` are all integral, so `pow(-2, 1e300)` is *not* a domain
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   error while `pow(-2, 2.5)` is.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Non-UTF-8 `argv`.** The diagnostics echo the raw bytes through `%s`, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   translation handles arguments as bytes; `String`-based handling would corrupt
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argc != 3` short-circuits everything.** Invalid arguments are never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   inspected when the count is wrong.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
